    "sectionId": "auto-repair:section:heating-cooling-system-repairs",
    "title": "Heater Core and Cooling System Repair",
    "body": "The heating system relies on the engine cooling system to transfer heat to the passenger cabin. Engine coolant flows through the heater core, and the HVAC blower motor forces air across the heated fins. Failures typically manifest as a lack of heat, a sweet smell in the cabin indicating a leak, or foggy windows. Diagnostic steps include checking coolant levels, measuring heater core inlet and outlet temperatures with a non-contact thermometer, and verifying heater control valve operation. If the inlet is hot but the outlet is cool, the heater core is likely restricted. Repair involves draining the cooling system, accessing the core, replacing it, and refilling/bleeding the cooling system to remove trapped air. Verification requires confirming no leaks exist under operating pressure and verifying heater vent temperatures match the manufacturer's specified range.",
